The owner decided on 2026-09-29 that re-baselining against the 2.0 plan is a standing part of the delivery loop (#1144). He has had to re-ground the work 10–20 times over two months. The latest case: on 2026-09-28 the coordinator spent about half a day fixing newly discovered defects (10 fixed, all real) while the #1163 release gate did not move.

**Triggers.** Re-baseline at whichever comes first:
- the start of a session or handoff;
- every 5 merged PRs;
- every schema-changing deploy batch;
- before starting any lane not on the current ordered critical path;
- a day without a #1163 acceptance row moving.

**A re-baseline means all of these:**
- reconcile the #1163 acceptance matrix against what shipped since the last re-baseline;
- close or re-scope stale issues;
- restate the ordered critical path to release on #1144;
- stamp the handoff plan's "Last rebaseline" line (date, merged-PR count since, critical path head);
- give the owner a short summary that says whether the recent work was on the plan or off it.

**Queue discipline.** Newly discovered defects are filed with a position in the ordered queue. They only jump it for data loss, capture loss or security, and the jump is stated explicitly.

Before ending a turn with a delegated Herdr agent still running, arm a one-shot completion watcher; its firing is a wake-up, never acceptance evidence.

Observed September 15, 2026 (session 01a08ba3). The parent agent delegated backup design to an Opus pane and ended its turn without a watcher. Opus finished, and the queue stalled until the user asked "where are we at? seems we are paused" (prompt 82a0540a-f2b3-558f-8226-93ab1fbc9b60) and "opus is done.. you should be monitoring" (primary prompt 4e3e24d5). The parent confirmed that nothing had been set to wake it.

The pattern that then worked (prompts 499f27e5, c95eb767, 9b23f774, d0a2cf22, c583a924, 2fbf8439, ca7b29bb): each delegated step arms a Herdr completion monitor that submits a single self-contained instruction to the parent and then exits. The instruction names:
- the PR and exact head SHA whose checks settled, or the artifact that finished;
- what to verify independently, for example "refresh the exact head, read the result, complete independent review", or "verify every artifact hash and database integrity; exit 0 alone is not artifact verification";
- what must not be redone, for example "do not reapply completed manifests";
- a standing clause: "preserve current work and real data … do not infer acceptance from completion alone."
Long-running lanes also get a periodic progress audit (for example every 20 minutes) that is explicitly "not acceptance evidence". When a pending delegated task is inspected, re-arm its monitor.

This complements pattern-55f78337 (Herdr-driven delivery lanes).
